      "body": "Thanks! Both crash fixes are right, and DSpark stays correct. The open point is speed on longer prompts.\r\n\r\nWith decoder SWA bounded replay, eager prefill runs layers 20-39 (of 40) only on each request's last `SWA_WINDOW` rows (the tail). Graph-run prefill runs them on all 8,192 rows of an 8k request, not 128.\r\n\r\nWe tested on MI355X (DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, TP4, DSpark). With random prompts, graphs cut median TTFT by 44% at 1k tokens and 25% at 2k. At 8k, it rises 70% (throughput -5%), and 64% with 4 requests at once (throughput -11%). On 92k-token prompts, results barely change: nearly all prefills there stay eager (over the 16K replay limit). Graph capture takes about 134 s and 22.4 GB per GPU. Startup takes about 64 s longer. GSM8K is unchanged.\r\n\r\nCUDA avoids this with `DecoderReplayTail.run_in_prefill_graph`. Until HIP has that, please use graphs only for prefill batches up to about 2,048 tokens. Either check this in the HIP `can_run_prefill_cuda_graph`, or document `--cuda-graph-max-bs-prefill 2048`.\r\n\r\nSmaller points:\r\n- The new `can_run_prefill_cuda_graph` repeats the runner's `max_seq_len` check, so it is not needed.\r\n- Attention DP (not tested): `late_layer_dp_counts` would get tail-row counts for graph steps that run all rows.\r\n- The `None` guards also hide a missing tail on CUDA. Please keep an assert there.\r\n- ruff-format wants a blank line before the new `def`.",
